  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 213 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nColorado remains sandwiched between an upper level high over the \nsouth central part of the county and a large trough off to the \nnorthwest. This will maintain southwest flow over Colorado and \ntransport subtropical moisture into the area. Precipitable water \nvalues are 150-160 percent of normal. Low level moisture has \nincreased since yesterday with dew points in the 50s across most \nof the urban corridor and northeast plains. This is yielding \nMLCAPE values of 500-1500 J/kg. Water vapor satellite imagery \nshows lift over the Great Basin associated with a jet streak. Over\nColorado, satellite imagery looks somewhat subsident. This lines \nup with model output, which shows isolated showers and \nthunderstorms later this afternoon and evening. Given the \ninstability and 0-6km bulk shear of 25-35 knots a couple of severe\nthunderstorms will be possible. Recent runs of the HRRR have \npushed convection further north near Fort Collins and the Cheyenne\nRidge while most of the other CAMs indicate at least the \npotential for isolated showers and storms in the urban corridor. \n\nOn Monday, the set-up will be similar to today's, with southwest \nflow aloft continuing between the ridge and trough. Flow aloft \nwill increase as the jet off to the west moves over the\u00a0Central \nRockies. The right entrance region of the jet slides over \nColorado, bringing an increase in cloud cover during the morning \nand likely providing lift for better coverage of thunderstorms \nMonday afternoon/evening. There is decent agreement that\u00a0the best \nlift ends up over the eastern plains. MLCAPE will be lower, below \n1000J/kg, which is expected to keep most or all of the \nthunderstorms sub-severe.\n\nAn upper level trough will track from the Northern Rockies to the\nNorthern Plains on Tuesday. This will flatten the ridge that's \nbeen over the region. The ridging shifts south, becoming elongated\nand stretching east-west across the southern part of the country.\nOver Colorado, we'll see zonal flow. A shortwave trough embedded \nin the flow aloft will travel across the region. The \nbetter\u00a0monsoon moisture begins to shift south of the area with\u00a0the\ndevelopment of the zonal. However, enough moisture is expected to\nlinger Tuesday for a round of afternoon/evening thunderstorms \nacross the urban corridor and eastern plains.\u00a0Cooler and drier air\nis expected for Wednesday behind the exiting shortwave and under \nthe zonal flow aloft. The cooling is subtle and high temperatures \nare still expected to be near to slightly above normal, with highs\nin the lower to mid 80s across northeast Colorado.\n\nZonal flow aloft continues Thursday and\u00a0Friday over the Central \nRockies. This pattern will keep monsoon moisture south of the area\nwith dry conditions expected both days. Temperatures warm up \nagain with high\u00a0temperatures across northeast Colorado reaching \nthe upper 80s and lower 90s.\n\nFor next weekend, eyes are on an upper-level trough/closed low \nforecast\u00a0to track across the northwest part of the country. So \nfar, models have shown very little run-to-run consistency with \neach model run being very different. On the bright side, at least \nthe models have trended toward a large upper level trough or \nclosed low. For Colorado, the biggest question is how far south \nthis system will dig. As it stands now, most models keep the \ntrough too far north for widespread precipitation. We will likely \nsee a cold front\u00a0push temperatures closer to normal. Given the \nforecast uncertainty, including a chance for showers in the \nforecast seems appropriate at this point.",
